const title       = 'How Do Ravens Raise Their Young?';
const description = 'Raven chicks hatch helpless and are raised by both parents over approximately 6 weeks in the nest, then continue to depend on parents for weeks after fledging. This page covers raven parental care, chick development, and when young ravens become independent.';
const path        = '/questions/how-do-ravens-raise-their-young/';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home',      url: '/' },
  { name: 'Questions', url: '/questions/' },
  { name: 'How Do Ravens Raise Their Young?', url: path },
];

const faq = [
  { q: 'How long do raven chicks stay in the nest?', a: 'Common Raven chicks remain in the nest for approximately 35–42 days after hatching (5–6 weeks). During this period they develop from helpless, blind, nearly naked hatchlings into fully feathered juveniles capable of flight. The nestling period in ravens is longer than in many similar-sized birds, reflecting the extended development time required for the raven\'s large brain.' },
  { q: 'Do both raven parents care for the young?', a: 'Yes. Both parents participate in raising the young, with clearly differentiated roles. The female does almost all incubation (20–21 days); the male feeds the female on the nest during incubation. After hatching, both parents feed the nestlings by regurgitation. The male typically brings more food to the nest; the female broods (keeps chicks warm) more in the early nestling period. After fledging, both parents continue to feed the young for 4–6 weeks while they develop foraging skills.' },
  { q: 'When do young ravens become independent?', a: 'Fledged young ravens (5–6 weeks after hatching) remain with their parents and continue to receive food for approximately 4–6 weeks post-fledging, before gradually becoming independent. They then typically join non-breeding flocks of juvenile and subadult ravens ("floaters"), where they spend 2–3 years before reaching sexual maturity and acquiring a breeding territory. Full independence from parental provisioning occurs roughly 2–3 months after hatching.' },
];
---

