    <h1>How Do Ravens Raise Their Young?</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Both parents raise raven chicks together. The female incubates eggs for 20–21 days while the male brings food. After hatching, both parents feed the nestlings for 35–42 days until fledging. Young ravens continue to receive food from parents for 4–6 weeks post-fledging, then join non-breeding flocks and spend 2–3 years as "floaters" before breeding.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Incubation</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Incubation in Common Ravens is primarily performed by the female. She begins incubating with the first or second egg laid, sitting continuously (with brief recesses) for approximately 20–21 days. The male's role during incubation is provisioning — he brings food to the female on the nest, often delivering it by regurgitation. The male also defends the nest territory and may sit on the nest briefly while the female feeds nearby, though this is less common than in some other species.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <p>Because incubation begins before all eggs are laid, hatching is asynchronous — the first egg hatches 1–2 days before the last. This means chicks in the same nest differ in age and size, with the first-hatched typically larger and more competitive for food.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Nestling Development</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Raven hatchlings are altricial — helpless at hatching, with eyes closed, little down, and unable to thermoregulate. Development is rapid:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Days 1–7:</strong> eyes open (around day 7–10); sparse dark natal down; completely dependent on female brooding for warmth.</li>
      <li><strong>Days 7–21:</strong> pin feathers emerge; primary wing feathers begin growing; audible begging calls increase in volume; both parents bring food by regurgitation.</li>
      <li><strong>Days 21–35:</strong> contour feathers emerge; thermoregulation improves; female broods less; both parents dramatically increase food delivery rate as chick growth accelerates.</li>
      <li><strong>Days 35–42:</strong> near-fully feathered; wing-flapping exercises begin; chicks move to nest edges and nearby perches; first flights by approximately day 38–42.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Feeding the Young</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Both parents feed nestlings by regurgitation — storing food in the oesophagus and throat pouch and delivering it directly to the chicks' open bills. Food delivery rates increase dramatically as chicks grow: by the third and fourth week, parents may make dozens of food deliveries per day. Food items include invertebrates, small vertebrates, carrion, and cached food retrieved from earlier stores.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <p>Heinrich's field studies showed that breeding pairs with established territories have significantly higher chick survival than pairs in marginal territories — food availability is the primary driver of nestling survival. In years with poor carrion availability, brood size at fledging is reduced, often with the smallest (last-hatched) chicks dying first.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Post-Fledging Care</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> After fledging (approximately 38–42 days after hatching), young ravens remain dependent on their parents for food for an additional 4–6 weeks. During this period they follow parents on foraging trips, observe foraging behaviour (a probable mechanism for social learning), and develop independent foraging skills. Siblings from the same clutch typically remain together and near the parental territory during this period.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <p>By late summer (for ravens breeding in spring), young ravens are largely independent and begin drifting away from the parental territory. They join non-breeding flocks of juvenile and subadult birds — the "floater" population described by Heinrich — where they spend 2–3 years before attempting to acquire a breeding territory and mate.</p>
  </section>

